# Creating this file makes Vercel stop reading .gitignore, so everything
# needed at runtime has to be explicitly allowed here (map.html loads
# src/building_story.js and the three data/ files below directly).

# Python offline data pipeline - never runs on Vercel, and its presence
# (esp. requirements.txt) is what made Vercel try to build a Python
# environment for what is actually a static site.
.venv/
requirements.txt
scripts/
notebooks/
src/*.py
.env
.env.example

# Not needed to serve the site
docs/
.claude/
README.md

# data/ holds many large intermediate/raw pipeline files (some 1GB+, same
# ones .gitignore keeps out of git) that must never reach the deploy. Matched
# by extension rather than a blanket "data/*" - confirmed via an actual build
# log (vercel inspect --logs) that "data/*" plus a negation for data/web/
# (tried both !data/web/ and !data/web/**, together and separately) still had
# Vercel's ignore matcher strip every file under data/web/. A glob like
# "data/*.json" can't match "data/web" at all (a directory, no extension), so
# data/web/ is never touched by these rules in the first place - no negation
# needed, nothing for that matcher bug to mishandle. data/web/ is the built
# output of scripts/build_web_data.py (per-borough building + footprint
# slices and the landing summary); map_dataset.json/footprints.json are its
# raw inputs and are correctly excluded by these same patterns.
data/*.csv
data/*.json
data/*.geojson
data/*.bak
data/*.superseded*
data/*.pre-geocode
data/*.pre-lens-metrics
data/sample2983_backup/
!data/neighborhoods.json
!data/nyc_boundary.geojson
